---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import NewsletterForm from '../components/NewsletterForm.astro';
import { EVENT_TYPES } from '../config/taxonomy';
import { href } from '../lib/paths';
import { site } from '../config/site';
---

<Base path="/about" title="About Us" description={site.description}>
	<div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-5 sm:px-8 py-14">
		<p class="label">About Us</p>
		<h1 class="mt-3 font-serif text-3xl sm:text-4xl leading-tight">{site.tagline}</h1>

		<div class="prose mt-8">
			{/* Opens with what used to be the homepage's "what this is" band. A
			    reader who wants the premise comes here for it; the homepage owes a
			    returning reader this week's issue instead. */}
			<p>
				<strong>{site.name}</strong> covers one question: when a geopolitical event happens, who pays
				for it, and through which mechanism?
			</p>
			<p>
				Not the politics — the transmission. Which price moves first, which moves late, and where the
				gap between the two is wide enough to matter.
			</p>
			<p>
				Most coverage of geopolitics stops at the event. Most coverage of markets starts at the
				price. {site.name} is about the part in between — the mechanism by which a decision taken in
				a capital city ends up in a portfolio, a freight bill or an inflation print.
			</p>

			<h2>What gets covered</h2>
			<p>
				Events are classified by the channel through which they transmit, not by the region they
				happened in:
			</p>
			<ul>
				{EVENT_TYPES.map((type) => <li>{type.label}</li>)}
			</ul>
			<p>
				Each issue is also tagged by region and by the asset classes it touches, which is what makes
				the <a href={href('/archive')}>archive</a> searchable along the dimensions that actually
				matter when you are looking something up months later.
			</p>

			<h2>How it works</h2>
			<p>
				One issue a week, {site.cadence.toLowerCase()}. When the next issue is published, the
				previous one moves into the archive automatically — same piece, same link, permanently. The
				newsletter and the database are not two libraries; they are the same library, and the
				newsletter is just the newest shelf.
			</p>
			<p>
				The <a href={href('/archive')}>archive</a> also holds <a href={href('/studies')}>studies</a>:
				standing pieces on how one country's economy transmits a shock, or on how a single
				mechanism works wherever it operates. An issue tells you what happened last week; a study
				is the background you would otherwise have to already know. They are searchable together,
				because a reader looking something up does not care which of the two answers them.
			</p>
			<p>
				The archive is free, has no account wall, and is searchable by full text as well as by
				region, asset class, event type and date — so the newsletter is not something you have to
				read the week it arrives.
			</p>

			<h2>Method, and its limits</h2>
			<p>
				Every issue lists its sources. Where a claim rests on an estimate rather than a reported
				figure, it is labelled as one. Where the read turns out to be wrong, the correction goes in
				a later issue rather than quietly into the original — the archive is a record, and a record
				you edit after the fact is not worth keeping.
			</p>
			<p>
				Studies work the opposite way, deliberately. They are reference rather than record, so they
				are revised as the facts change and each one shows the date it was last revised. A piece on
				a country's debt that is frozen at the day it was written is worse than no piece. The rule
				is the same underneath both: you should always be able to tell how old the claim in front
				of you is.
			</p>

			<h2>Not investment advice</h2>
			<p>
				This is analysis and commentary for general information. It is not investment advice, not a
				recommendation to buy or sell anything, and takes no account of your circumstances. Figures
				are believed accurate at the time of writing and are not updated afterwards.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div class="mt-12">
			<NewsletterForm />
		</div>
	</div>
</Base>
